: \"unknown\",\n      \"validated_state_message\": null,\n      \"visible\": true\n    },\n    {\n      \"create_time\": \"2026-08-17T11:34:16.655091\",\n      \"data_type\": \"galaxy.datatypes.tabular.Tabular\",\n      \"deleted\": false,\n      \"file_ext\": \"tabular\",\n      \"file_size\": 0,\n      \"genome_build\": \"?\",\n      \"hda_ldda\": \"hda\",\n      \"hid\": 7,\n      \"history_content_type\": \"dataset\",\n      \"history_id\": \"bbd44e69cb8906b50696dbe9526b791f\",\n      \"id\": \"f9cad7b01a47213502ba913640c121ab\",\n      \"metadata_column_names\": [],\n      \"metadata_column_types\": [],\n      \"metadata_columns\": 0,\n      \"metadata_comment_lines\": 0,\n      \"metadata_data_lines\": 0,\n      \"metadata_dbkey\": \"?\",\n      \"metadata_delimiter\": \"\\t\",\n      \"misc_blurb\": \"queued\",\n      \"misc_info\": null,\n      \"model_class\": \"HistoryDatasetAssociation\",\n      \"name\": \"Busco on dataset 1: Full table - Specific lineage\",\n      \"output_name\": \"busco_table\",\n      \"peek\": null,\n      \"purged\": false,\n      \"state\": \"new\",\n      \"tags\": [],\n      \"update_time\": \"2026-08-17T11:34:16.655092\",\n      \"uuid\": \"cc4f8c77-0073-401d-aff1-4cee4d554da6\",\n      \"validated_state\": \"unknown\",\n      \"validated_state_message\": null,\n      \"visible\": true\n    }\n  ],\n  \"produces_entry_points\": false\n}\n--- validation response\n{\n  \"action\": \"/tool_runner/index\",\n  \"citations\": true,\n  \"creator\": null,\n  \"credentials\": [],\n  \"description\": \"Assess genome assembly and annotation completeness\",\n  \"display\": true,\n  \"edam_operations\": [],\n  \"edam_topics\": [],\n  \"enctype\": \"application/x-www-form-urlencoded\",\n  \"errors\": {},\n  \"form_style\": \"regular\",\n  \"has_parameters\": true,\n  \"help\": \"<div class=\\\"section\\\" id=\\\"busco-assessing-genome-assembly-and-annotation-completeness-with-benchmarking-universal-single-copy-orthologs\\\">\\n<h1>BUSCO: Assessing genome assembly and annotation completeness with Benchmarking Universal Single-Copy Orthologs</h1>\\n<div class=\\\"section\\\" id=\\\"interpreting-the-results\\\">\\n<h2>Interpreting the results</h2>\\n<p><a class=\\\"reference external\\\" href=\\\"http://busco.ezlab.org/\\\">BUSCO</a> attempts to provide a quantitative assessment of the completeness in terms of the expected gene content of a\\ngenome assembly, transcriptome, or annotated gene set. The results are simplified into categories of Complete\\nand single-copy, Complete and duplicated, Fragmented, or Missing BUSCOs.</p>\\n<p>BUSCO completeness results make sense only in the context of the biology of your organism.\\nYou have to understand whether missing or duplicated genes are of biological or technical origin.\\nFor instance, a high level of duplication may be explained by a recent whole duplication event\\n(biological) or a chimeric assembly of haplotypes (technical).\\nTranscriptomes and protein sets that are not filtered for isoforms will lead to a high proportion of duplicates.\\nTherefore you should filter them before a BUSCO analysis.\\nFinally, focusing on specific tissues or specific life stages and conditions in a transcriptomic experiment\\nis unlikely to produce a BUSCO-complete transcriptome. In this case, consistency across your samples\\nis what you will be aiming for.</p>\\n<p>For more information please refer to the <a class=\\\"reference external\\\" href=\\\"http://busco.ezlab.org/\\\">Busco</a> <a class=\\\"reference external\\\" href=\\\"https://busco.ezlab.org/busco_userguide.html#interpreting-the-results\\\">user guide</a>\\n.</p>\\n</div>\\n</div>\\n\",\n  \"help_format\": \"restructuredtext\",\n  \"hidden\": \"\",\n  \"hidden_versions\": [],\n  \"history_id\": \"bbd44e69cb8906b50696dbe9526b791f\",\n  \"icon\": null,\n  \"id\": \"toolshed.g2.bx.psu.edu/repos/iuc/busco/busco/5.8.0+galaxy2\",\n  \"inputs\": [\n    {\n      \"argument\": null,\n      \"default_value\": null,\